import Native

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The vector partition - Analysis-Avx512.md §4.7
   ==========================================================================================

   `Sort.yana` is the sort; this is one step of it written for a container whose element is a lane.
   Everything else is that file's and is deliberately untouched: the insertion sort for a short
   range, the heap fallback, and the recursion budget that decides when the fallback is due. §4.7
   is explicit that the budget is a worst-case argument which does not care how a partition is
   computed, and this file is the demonstration of that.

   ## What a vector partition is

   A block is compared with the pivot once, and `compress` then packs the lanes that belong on each
   side into the bottom of a register - so one register's worth of elements is placed with two
   comparisons' worth of work and two stores, where Hoare's scan is a comparison and a branch per
   element. The stores are `storeUpTo`, which writes exactly the lanes that were kept (§3.5): a
   whole-register store would reach past the free region into elements nobody has read yet.

   ## Why it can run in place

   The free region is `[lStore, rStore + width)`, and it never shrinks below a register: the loop
   reads its next block from whichever end has *less* room behind it, so the block it is about to
   overwrite has already been loaded. The first and last registers of the range are held in
   registers for the whole walk for the same reason - their slots are the first the loop writes -
   and are partitioned at the end. That is the published in-place scheme (Intel's `x86-simd-sort`,
   and Highway's `vqsort` under a different name); what is written here is its structure, not its
   code.

   ## Equal elements, which is where a partition of this shape goes wrong

   The split is `< pivot` against `>= pivot`, so a range whose every element equals the pivot puts
   nothing on the left and makes no progress at all. Two things answer that, and both are cheap:

   - **The smallest and the biggest of the range are tracked while it is partitioned**, as a vector
     minimum and maximum that cost one instruction each per block. A pivot equal to the biggest
     means everything at or above it *is* it, so that side is already sorted and is not recursed
     into; a pivot equal to the smallest means the left side is empty.
   - **A range that made no progress is partitioned again as `<= pivot`**, which puts every element
     equal to the pivot on the left and leaves a strictly smaller range on the right. The pivot is
     one of the range's own elements, so that side always shrinks.

   Together those turn the all-equal range - and the two-distinct-value array, which median-of-three
   walks straight into - from a budget burn into one pass.
-}

{-
   One register partitioned into the two ends of the free region `[left, right)`.

   The lanes that stay below go to the bottom of it and the rest to the top, each with a
   `storeUpTo` of exactly its own count, so the region shrinks by a whole register from both ends
   at once. The answer is how many went up, which is what the caller moves `rStore` by.
-}
@inline fn (Ord(a), TrivialCopy(a), Lanewise(Vec(a), Mask(a)), Bitwise(Mask(a)))
    placeOneBlock(&xs: [a], left: Size, right: Size, curr: Vec(a), pivot: Vec(a), orEqual: Bool,
                  &small: Vec(a), &big: Vec(a)) -> Int:
    let below = if orEqual then not(pivot .< curr) else curr .< pivot

    let {down, stays} = compress(curr, below)
    let {up, moves} = compress(curr, not(below))

    storeUpTo(xs.items + left, stays, down)
    storeUpTo(xs.items + (right - (moves :: Size)), moves, up)

    small = min(small, curr)
    big = max(big, curr)
    return moves

{-
   The range `[first, last]` partitioned about `pivot` - where the upper side begins, and the
   smallest and biggest elements the walk saw.

   `orEqual` picks which side an element equal to the pivot goes to, and the two uses of it are the
   note above: `False` is the ordinary split and `True` is the retry that guarantees progress.

   The odd elements at the front are placed one at a time first, so that what is left is a whole
   number of registers - which is what lets every step below be a register with no tail.
-}
fn (Ord(a), TrivialCopy(a), Lanewise(Vec(a), Mask(a)), Bitwise(Mask(a)))
   partitionLanes(&xs: [a], first: Size, last: Size, pivot: a, orEqual: Bool) -> {Size, a, a}:
    let width = lanes(zero() :: Vec(a)) :: Size

    let &left = first
    let &right = last + 1
    let &smallest = xs[first]
    let &biggest = xs[first]

    let &extra = (right - left) % width
    while extra > 0:
        let value = xs[left]
        if value < smallest then smallest = value
        if biggest < value then biggest = value

        if (if orEqual then !(pivot < value) else value < pivot):
            left = left + 1
        else:
            right = right - 1
            swap(xs[left], xs[right])

        extra = extra - 1

    if left == right then return {left, smallest, biggest}

    let pivots = splat(pivot) :: Vec(a)
    let &small = splat(smallest) :: Vec(a)
    let &big = splat(biggest) :: Vec(a)

    if right - left == width:
        let only = loadVector(xs, left)
        let moves = placeOneBlock(xs, left, left + width, only, pivots, orEqual, small, big)
        return {left + width - (moves :: Size), horizontalMin(small), horizontalMax(big)}

    -- The two registers whose slots the walk writes first, held for the whole of it.
    let held = loadVector(xs, left)
    let heldLast = loadVector(xs, right - width)

    let &lStore = left
    let &rStore = right - width
    left = left + width
    right = right - width

    while right != left:
        -- From whichever end has less room behind it, so the block read is one the stores have not
        -- reached yet.
        let fromTop = (rStore + width) - right < left - lStore
        if fromTop then right = right - width

        let curr = loadVector(xs, if fromTop then right else left)
        if !fromTop then left = left + width

        let moves = placeOneBlock(xs, lStore, rStore + width, curr, pivots, orEqual, small, big)
        rStore = rStore - (moves :: Size)
        lStore = lStore + width - (moves :: Size)

    let fromHead = placeOneBlock(xs, lStore, rStore + width, held, pivots, orEqual, small, big)
    lStore = lStore + width - (fromHead :: Size)

    let fromTail = placeOneBlock(xs, lStore, lStore + width, heldLast, pivots, orEqual, small, big)
    lStore = lStore + width - (fromTail :: Size)

    return {lStore, horizontalMin(small), horizontalMax(big)}

{-
   Whether the range is already in order, and whether it is exactly against it.

   **This is the one thing the vector partition needs that the scalar one does not.** Hoare's scan
   over a range that is already sorted does no swaps at all and takes the same branch every time, so
   a processor runs it at close to a comparison a cycle; the partition here does its full work
   whatever the data, because that is what "branch-free" means. Measured over 200,000 `Int`s, the
   vector sort is 2.3x the scalar one on random input and 5x on an input of five distinct values -
   and was **seven times slower** on an input that arrived sorted, which is not a rare shape: a list
   re-sorted after one append, a merge of two ordered runs, a key column already in order.

   A scan answers it. Both tests read a register against the register one element behind it, so a
   range that is out of order at its first pair costs one comparison and a range that is in order
   costs a pass with no branch in it - which is a fraction of what the partition it replaces would
   have cost. `sorted` returns outright and `descending` reverses and returns, so either answer
   settles the whole range rather than one level of it.

   The test is made at every level and not once at the top, which is what makes a *partly* ordered
   input cheap too: the ordered halves are found as the recursion reaches them. What it costs in
   return is one failed scan in front of each partition, and a failed scan stops at the first pair
   that is out of order.
-}
fn (Ord(a), Lanewise(Vec(a), Mask(a))) ascendingRange(xs: [a], low: Size, high: Size) -> Bool:
    let width = lanes(zero() :: Vec(a)) :: Size
    let &i = low + 1

    while i + width <= high + 1:
        if any(loadVector(xs, i) .< loadVector(xs, i - 1)) then return False
        i = i + width

    while i <= high:
        if xs[i] < xs[i - 1] then return False
        i = i + 1

    return True

fn (Ord(a), Lanewise(Vec(a), Mask(a))) descendingRange(xs: [a], low: Size, high: Size) -> Bool:
    let width = lanes(zero() :: Vec(a)) :: Size
    let &i = low + 1

    while i + width <= high + 1:
        if any(loadVector(xs, i - 1) .< loadVector(xs, i)) then return False
        i = i + width

    while i <= high:
        if xs[i - 1] < xs[i] then return False
        i = i + 1

    return True

-- A range turned end for end, which is what a non-increasing one has to be to be sorted. Element by
-- element and by swapping, as everything in `Sort.yana` is: a vector reversal would have to be a
-- shuffle per register and a pair of stores, and this runs once per range rather than per level.
fn (Ord(a)) reverseRange(&xs: [a], low: Size, high: Size):
    let &i = low
    let &j = high

    while i < j:
        swap(xs[i], xs[j])
        i = i + 1
        j = j - 1

-- The median of the range's two ends and its middle, as a value: `partitionRange` parks the same
-- three at `low` because its scan reads the pivot out of the container, and this one does not.
fn (Ord(a), TrivialCopy(a)) pivotOf(xs: [a], low: Size, high: Size) -> a:
    let mid = low + (high - low) / 2
    let head = xs[low]
    let middle = xs[mid]
    let tail = xs[high]

    if head < middle:
        if middle < tail then return middle
        return if head < tail then tail else head

    if head < tail then return head
    return if middle < tail then tail else middle

{-
   `sortRange` with the vector partition in it.

   The shape is that function's, down to the tail call on the larger side and the recursion on the
   smaller: what is different is the partition, the two skips the tracked extremes allow, and the
   floor - every range shorter than two registers goes to `sortRange`, which is where the insertion
   sort and the scalar partition still live.
-}
fn (Ord(a), TrivialCopy(a), Lanewise(Vec(a), Mask(a)), Bitwise(Mask(a)))
   sortRangeLanes(&xs: [a], first: Size, last: Size, depth: Int):
    let width = lanes(zero() :: Vec(a)) :: Size
    let &low = first
    let &high = last
    let &budget = depth

    while high - low + 1 >= width + width:
        -- A range that is already in order, or exactly against it, is what a branch-free partition
        -- is worst at and what a scan is best at - see `ascendingRange`.
        if ascendingRange(xs, low, high) then return
        if descendingRange(xs, low, high):
            reverseRange(xs, low, high)
            return

        if budget <= 0:
            heapRange(xs, low, high)
            return

        budget = budget - 1

        let pivot = pivotOf(xs, low, high)
        let {at, smallest, biggest} = partitionLanes(xs, low, high, pivot, False)

        if pivot == smallest:
            -- Nothing is below the pivot. If nothing is above it either the range is one value and
            -- is already in order; otherwise the equals are moved to the front so that what is left
            -- is strictly smaller.
            if pivot == biggest then return

            let {again, _, _} = partitionLanes(xs, low, high, pivot, True)
            low = again
        else if pivot == biggest:
            -- Everything at or above the pivot is the pivot, so only the lower side is left.
            high = at - 1
        else if at - low < high - at:
            sortRangeLanes(xs, low, at - 1, budget)
            low = at
        else:
            sortRangeLanes(xs, at, high, budget)
            high = at - 1

    sortRange(xs, low, high, budget)
